course: dictionary
lesson: 8496
title: Two meanings of one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence show you which meaning the writer means. «After blowing your nose or coughing into your hands you wash or sanitise them, so cold germs do not spread.» The clues are germs and hands, so sanitise means to clean something very well so it is safe.
explain: The second meaning is about stories and reports. To sanitise a story is to take out the unpleasant parts so it seems nicer than the truth. If a report says the trip was cold, muddy and the bus broke down, and someone sanitises it, the cold, the mud and the broken bus are left out.
explain: Ask yourself: is this about germs and cleaning, or about changing a story? Then test your meaning in the sentence. If "clean very well to kill germs" fits, it is the first meaning. If "leave out the bad bits" fits, it is the second.
fact: Sanitise means to clean something very well, killing germs, so it is safe and hygienic.
fact: «Sanitise your hands» means clean them, often with hand gel, so germs do not spread.
fact: To sanitise a story or report means to take out the unpleasant parts so it seems nicer than the truth.
fact: Sanitise is a verb. Its other forms are sanitises, sanitised and sanitising.
fact: In American English the spelling is sanitize.
exercise: «Wash or sanitise your hands after you cough.» What does sanitise mean here? | To dry them with a towel | To make them wet and cold | To hide them in your pockets | *To clean very well, killing germs
exercise: A report says, «The trip was cold, muddy and the bus broke down.» Ben sanitises it. What does he do? | *He takes out the unpleasant parts so it sounds nicer | He writes it in capital letters | He copies it word for word | He adds more mud and rain
exercise: Which phrase names a gel that cleans hands and kills germs? | hand-drawn map | hand cream | *hand sanitiser | hand puppet
test: Mia asked the class to ___ their hands before lunch to stop germs spreading. | sketch | sing | sprint | *sanitise
test: Ravi wants to ___ the rude bits out of his story before he reads it to his gran. | *sanitise | sprinkle | sneeze | sparkle
exam: Which sentence uses sanitise to mean taking out the unpleasant parts? | *She sanitised the story, leaving out the storm and the broken boat | The dog sanitised the ball by running off with it | She sanitised her shoes and went out to play | They sanitised the sandwich by adding more cheese
